  "prediction": "The main discussion points of the meeting included the following:\n\n1. The Meeting Recorder project, including the collection of audio data from meetings, the transcription of the data, and the analysis of the data to study speech patterns and interactions.\n2. The challenges of transcribing the data, including the need for manual marking of speaker overlaps and the difficulty of distinguishing between different types of acoustic events.\n3. The use of close-talking mikes versus far-field mikes, and the potential for using a combination of both to improve the quality of the data.\n4. The development of a program to automatically detect speaker overlaps from the close-talking mikes, and the potential for using this program to speed up the transcription process.\n5. The collection of data from other meetings, including those from the natural language group and the network services and applications group, and the potential for recording additional meetings to increase the size of the corpus.\n6. The use of data from local broadcast media, including radio shows and panel discussions, to supplement the corpus and provide a more diverse range of data.\n7. The potential for using the corpus to study a range of research questions, including the patterns of energy and interaction in meetings, and the use of the corpus to develop and test speech recognition systems.\n8. The logistics of collecting and transcribing the data, including the need for a large amount of time and resources, and the potential for using student labor to help with the transcription process.\n9. The use of the corpus to study the effects of echo cancellation and other audio processing techniques on the quality of the data.\n10. The potential for using the corpus to develop and test new speech recognition systems, and the need for further research and development to improve the quality and accuracy of the data.",
